=====Dataproc Cluster Prerequisites & Integration================
-------------------------------------------------------------+
|  🔑 Prerequisites                                            |
+-------------------------------------------------------------+
| 1. Enable Billing                                            |
|    - Why: Needed to pay for VMs, storage, and jobs.          |
|    - Without it: Cluster creation fails.                     |
|                                                             |
| 2. Enable Dataproc API                                       |
|    - Why: Activates Dataproc service in your project.        |
|    - Without it: Requests to create clusters are blocked.    |
|                                                             |
| 3. IAM Roles / Permissions                                   |
|    - Why: Controls who can create/manage clusters.           |
|    - Without it: "Permission denied" errors.                 |
|                                                             |
| 4. Choose Region                                             |
|    - Why: Ensures data locality, reduces latency & cost.     |
|    - Wrong choice: Slower jobs + higher network charges.     |
|                                                             |
| 5. Networking Setup                                          |
|    - Why: Nodes need a VPC to talk to each other & GCP.      |
|    - Without it: Cluster cannot communicate internally.      |
|                                                             |
| 6. Service Account                                           |
|    - Why: Identity for cluster to access GCP services.       |
|    - Without it: Jobs fail to read/write data.               |
+-------------------------------------------------------------+
*************Integration with GCP & On-Prem************************************8
+-------------------------------------------------------------+
|  🌐 Integration                                              |
+-------------------------------------------------------------+
| Default: Cloud Storage (GCS)                                |
| - Works out-of-the-box, no setup needed.                     |
|                                                             |
| Other GCP Services (BigQuery, Pub/Sub, Cloud SQL)           |
| - Need IAM roles + connectors.                              |
| - Configured after cluster creation.                        |
|                                                             |
| On-Prem Databases                                           |
| - Need VPN/Interconnect or firewall rules.                  |
| - Install JDBC drivers via init actions.                    |
| - Provide connection strings in job configs.                 |
+-------------------------------------------------------------+

Q1: Why do we need to enable billing before creating a cluster?
A1: Dataproc clusters run on Compute Engine VMs, use Cloud Storage, and may connect to BigQuery.
All of these are paid services. Without billing enabled, 
Google Cloud won’t let you provision resources because 
there’s no way to charge for them. Think of it like activating a SIM card—you can’t make calls until billing is active.

Q2: Why must the Dataproc API be enabled?
A2: Every GCP service (Dataproc, BigQuery, Pub/Sub, etc.) is controlled via an API.
The Dataproc API allows you to create, manage, and delete clusters programmatically. 
If it’s not enabled, your cluster creation request will fail 
because GCP doesn’t know you want to use Dataproc. 
It’s like turning on the “switch” that allows your project to talk to Dataproc.

Q3: Why are IAM roles / permissions important?
A3: GCP uses IAM (Identity and Access Management) to control who can do what.
To create a cluster, you need roles like Owner, Editor, or
Dataproc Admin. Without proper permissions, even if billing and API are enabled, 
you’ll get “Permission denied” errors. 
This ensures only authorized people can spin up clusters (important for cost and security control).

Q4: Why do we choose a region carefully?
A4: Dataproc clusters are tied to a region (e.g., asia-south1 for Mumbai). 
Your cluster must be in the same region as your data (Cloud Storage bucket, 
BigQuery dataset) for low latency and reduced costs. If you choose a region far away

Q5: Why is networking setup required?
A5: Clusters need a VPC (Virtual Private Cloud) to communicate internally and with other GCP services.
By default, GCP provides a “default” network, but in production you may need custom networks for security and isolation.
Without networking, your cluster nodes can’t talk to each other or access storage. 
It’s like building roads before you can move trucks between warehouses.

Q6: Why do we assign a service account?
A6: A service account is the identity your cluster uses to access other GCP services
(like reading from Cloud Storage or writing to BigQuery). 
If you don’t assign the right service account, your jobs may fail because 
the cluster can’t access the data. This is the “digital ID card” for your cluster.

Q7: Do we need to configure integration with GCP services or on-prem DBs during cluster creation?
A7: No. Cluster creation is kept simple. 
GCS works out-of-the-box. Other GCP services need IAM roles + connectors, 
which you can add later. On-prem DBs require networking (VPN/Interconnect or firewall rules) and JDBC drivers,
usually installed via init actions after the cluster is up.

Q8: If data resides in an on-prem DB, will processing be slower?
A8: Yes. 
Because the cluster is in GCP and the DB is on-prem, 
data must travel across WAN links. This adds latency, bandwidth limits, and costs. 
Best practice is to migrate or replicate data into GCP storage (like GCS or BigQuery) 
before running heavy Spark/Hadoop jobs. That way compute and data are co-located, ensuring faster and cheaper processing.


